870 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 676 0 R
/T (I. Literature Review)
/E (I. Literature Review)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
871 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 676 0 R
/E (A. Understanding Sustainability Measurement)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
872 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 676 0 R
/E (Recent literature distinguishes sustainability measurement into two main approaches: sustainability disclosure and reporting frameworks, and ESG ratings. The former refers to any structured set of principles, goals, and guidelines that guide organizations and entities in disclosing the ESG considerations integrated into their strategies and operations. These frameworks are typically principles-based, and aim to shape corporate behaviors by providing direction on what ESG topics to address and how entities can contribute to broader societal goals \(van Tulder & van Mil, 2022\). )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
873 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 676 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
874 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 676 0 R
/Pg 15 0 R
/A [4010 0 R 0]
/K [10]
>>
endobj
875 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 677 0 R
/E (7)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
876 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 677 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
877 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 677 0 R
/E (This creates an additional and compounding barrier to widespread sustainability integration amongst SMEs, while also contributing to broader skepticism regarding the usefulness, comparability, and applicability of ESG ratings and sustainability disclosure systems.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
878 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 677 0 R
/E (B. Overarching Structure of Performance Measurement)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
879 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 677 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
880 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 677 0 R
/E (A second common feature is the use of indicator-based systems. ESG frameworks do not rely on a single metric \(Berg et al., 2022\). Instead, they aggregate multiple indicators into pillar-level or overall scores. For example, Refinitiv derives ESG scores from more than 450 metrics, which are grouped into categories and then aggregated into environmental, social, and governance pillars. Similarly, MSCI ratings are built from ESG themes and key issues, with scores weighted according to industry-specific exposure and materiality.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
881 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 677 0 R
/E (C. Disclosure Frameworks vs. Rating Systems)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
882 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 677 0 R
/E (Ratings and frameworks serve different purposes in sustainability measurement. Ratings provide a comparative assessment, showing how a company performs relative to peers or risk categories, while reporting frameworks focus on what information should be disclosed, how it is structured, and for whom. For instance, EcoVadis provides scorecards and badges, MSCI uses industry-relative letter grades, and Sustainalytics assigns unmanaged-risk categories, whereas frameworks such as GRI, IFRS S1/S2, and TCFD define disclosure requirements rather than rankings.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
