/E (F. Integration of Quantitative and Qualitative Indicators in Ratings & Frameworks)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
937 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 681 0 R
/E (Importantly, sustainability performance evaluation asses both quantitative metrics and qualitative inputs. Quantitative indicators include measurable data, such as greenhouse gas \(GHG\) emissions, energy consumption, waste generation, and employment statistics. At the same time, qualitative inputs capture dimensions that are not directly measurable, such as corporate policies, governance structures, risk management processes, and stakeholder engagement practices. The integration of both types of data reflects the multidimensional nature of sustainability, where performance outcomes must be understood alongside organizational processes and strategic intent \(Islam et al., 2023\).)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
938 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 681 0 R
/E (Furthermore, sustainability frameworks incorporate elements of materiality and contextual prioritization, recognizing that not all indicators are equally relevant across industries. The study demonstrates that material factors are often categorized and prioritized based on stakeholder expectations and sector-specific considerations, as seen in the electricity utility sector where environmental, social, and governance factors are weighted differently depending on their perceived importance to customers and operational impacts \(Islam et al., 2023\). This highlights the role of industry context in shaping how sustainability indicators are selected and evaluated.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
939 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 681 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
