956 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 683 0 R
/E (Waste usually comes from operational activities that reflect the growing recognition of the environmental, economic, and social costs of inefficient resource use. In the food industry specifically, waste takes on significance in scale of food loss and waste globally. Gustavsson et al. \(2011\) estimate that approximately one-third of all food produced for human consumption, roughly 1.3 billion tonnes per year, is lost or wasted along the supply chain, which is a significant environmental burden in terms of land use, water consumption, and GHG emissions. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
957 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 683 0 R
/E (Water)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
958 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 683 0 R
/E (Water is emerging as one of the most critical and contested operational metrics in sustainability assessment, driven by growing concerns about global water scarcity and the disproportionate water footprint of industrial food production. Mekonnen and Hoekstra \(2016\) find that approximately four billion people face severe water scarcity for at least one month per year, a figure that is projected to worsen significantly under climate change scenarios. This context gives corporate water management metrics considerable urgency, particularly for companies operating in water-stressed regions or relying on water-intensive agricultural supply chains.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
