/S /P
/P 685 0 R
/E (Despite their growing adoption, sustainability disclosure frameworks and ESG rating systems face several broader structural limitations identified throughout sustainability assessment literature. Existing frameworks frequently apply differing indicators, methodologies, scoring systems, and assumptions, making sustainability performance difficult to compare consistently across organizations, sectors, and geographic contexts. Chatterji et al. \(2016\) identify divergence across ESG ratings in terms of scope, measurement methodologies, and weighting approaches, while Gibson, Krueger and Schmidt \(2021\) argue that such inconsistencies ultimately reflect the absence of a universally agreed-upon definition of corporate sustainability itself.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
984 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 685 0 R
/E (The literature also highlights an inherent trade-off between comprehensiveness and usability. More comprehensive disclosure systems often require substantial time, technical expertise, data availability, and financial resources to implement effectively, while simplified approaches may sacrifice consistency, comparability, and analytical depth. Frameworks additionally tend to rely on standardized baselines and universal disclosure objectives that may not fully reflect local operating conditions, sector-specific realities, or organization-specific constraints, reducing practical relevance in real-world implementation contexts \(Ibrahim et al., 2025\).)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
985 0 obj
<<
