>>
endobj
988 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 686 0 R
/E (Collectively, these limitations have contributed to ongoing debate regarding the transparency, comparability, practical utility, and real-world applicability of existing sustainability disclosure and ESG rating systems.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
989 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 686 0 R
/E (B. The Burden of Disparate Standards on SMEs)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
990 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 686 0 R
/E (There is no shortage of sustainability frameworks or ratings. Rather, the most widely adopted sustainability disclosure frameworks and ESG rating systems were not built with SMEs in mind. As mentioned above, disclosure guidelines such as GRI, SASB/ISSB, and TCFD, alongside third-party ESG ratings including EcoVadis, MSCI, and Sustainalytics, have historically focused on investors and larger public companies with established governance processes, sustainability teams, and internal systems capable of producing sustainability disclosures \(UNCTAD, 2024\). However, SMEs are increasingly finding themselves pulled into these systems downstream through buyer requirements, lender screening protocols, procurement processes, and supply chain disclosure requests despite not having been a primary stakeholder in the design of the frameworks they are now expected to navigate.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
991 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 686 0 R
/E (This mismatch becomes particularly evident when considering organizational capacity and data availability. Most SMEs do not maintain dedicated sustainability teams, with responsibility for sustainability activities often added onto existing operational or administrative roles and deprioritized relative to immediate business needs. SMEs also frequently lack internal systems capable of systematically tracking environmental and social performance metrics, making sustainability reporting a highly manual and time-intensive process. In a global survey of more than 16,000 SMEs across industries, 73% cited high initial costs as the largest barrier to sustainability reporting, while only 8% reported currently producing sustainability disclosures despite 83% stating that sustainability was important to their business overall \(Sage, 2023\). The gap between SME-level sustainability impact and formal reporting therefore appears less attributable to lack of interest than to the absence of systems, resources, and reporting structures around which existing frameworks were designed.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
992 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 686 0 R
/T (SPRING 2026 CAPSTONE REPORT - SECTION 008)
/E (SPRING 2026 CAPSTONE REPORT - SECTION 008)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
