/P 687 0 R
/E (17)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
995 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 687 0 R
/E (A second challenge relates to the complexity and opacity of existing sustainability frameworks and ESG rating systems. This challenge is compounded by the fact that many sustainability frameworks and rating systems tend to favor formalization and disclosure capacity over demonstrated operational performance. SMEs that engage in sustainable operational practices but lack formal documentation, disclosure infrastructure, or dedicated reporting resources may therefore be disadvantaged relative to organizations capable of producing extensive sustainability reports, policies, and procedural documentation. This dynamic can unintentionally incentivize visible reporting and disclosure activity over substantive operational improvement and measurable sustainability outcomes.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
996 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 687 0 R
/E (Fragmentation across sustainability requests further compounds these challenges. SMEs are increasingly required to respond to overlapping sustainability information requests from buyers, banks, certification schemes, regulators, and supply chain partners, often across differing formats and reporting expectations. There is currently no universally adopted baseline sustainability dataset that SMEs can maintain and reuse efficiently across requests, meaning organizations frequently duplicate similar reporting efforts across multiple stakeholders and platforms. This fragmented approach consumes limited organizational capacity while delivering limited incremental value to either SMEs or requesting parties.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
997 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 687 0 R
/E (Collectively, these barriers create broader systemic consequences extending beyond individual firms. SMEs may become excluded from procurement opportunities and supply chains due to inability to satisfy disclosure requirements rather than underlying operational sustainability performance. Reporting burdens also tend to fall disproportionately on smaller organizations with fewer resources, contributing to uneven sustainability adoption across markets and increasing the risk of performative sustainability practices in which organizations prioritize visible disclosure activity over meaningful operational change. Without more SME-appropriate sustainability assessment methods, the very mechanisms designed to accelerate sustainability progress risk concentrating it among large corporations, leaving the 90% of global businesses - SMEs - struggling to participate \(United Nations, n.d.\).)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
998 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 687 0 R
/E (SPRING 2026 CAPSTONE REPORT - SECTION 008)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
999 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 687 0 R
/T (III. The Solution: Synthesizing Sustainability for SMEs)
/E (III. The Solution: Synthesizing Sustainability for SMEs)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1000 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 687 0 R
/E (If existing sustainability frameworks are overly complex, fragmented, and difficult for SMEs to operationalize, then the solution is not to build yet another comprehensive framework, but rather a sustainability structure built around simplicity, usability, and applicability. In other words, SMEs need a synthesized sustainability framework that harmonizes and simplifies existing standards. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
