1004 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 688 0 R
/E (A. A Synthesized Sustainability Framework for SMEs)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1005 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 688 0 R
/E (Drawing on the gaps and barriers discussed in Section 2.1, we identified four key considerations to guide our approach to building a synthesized sustainability framework for SMEs. First, the framework centers on curating a minimum viable dataset of indicators - indicators that are feasible for SMEs to report, are reportable on a repeatable basis, and can be actionably improved upon over time. This meant limiting scope to what is actually material to SMEs and their buyers, and excluding indicators unlikely to be actionable or decision-useful in practice.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1006 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 688 0 R
/E (Second, we adopted a layered architecture in which a baseline of universal indicators applies across all firms, while sector-, geography-, and market-specific indicators are added based on the context of the reporting SME. This approach enables comparability across companies while still accounting for meaningful variation in sustainability risks across sectors and geographies.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1007 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 688 0 R
/E (Third, scoring logic is transparent and rules-based, allowing both SMEs and buyers to understand precisely how a score is calculated and how it can be improved, thereby improving transparency and making scores easier for SMEs and buyers to interpret and act upon. Fourth, the framework emphasizes progression. Scores are tiered \(i.e. foundation, advanced, etc.\) to highlight improvement over time. Designing the system this way rewards operational improvement and practice adoption over box-checking alone.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1008 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 688 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1009 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 688 0 R
/T (B. Procurement-Focused Sustainability)
/E (B. Procurement-Focused Sustainability)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1010 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 688 0 R
/E (A critical differentiator of our solution is that it reframes sustainability through the lens of procurement rather than investor disclosure. Existing sustainability systems were largely designed to support capital allocation, regulatory disclosure, and reputational risk management for large corporations. However, in the SME context, the more immediate business driver is often procurement: remaining eligible, competitive, and attractive as a supplier. Given that close to 50% of B2B buyers expect to exclude suppliers that do not meet minimum sustainability criteria in the next three years \(Bain & Company, 2025\), many SMEs are increasingly faced with the practical question of how to remain viable participants within evolving supply chains.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1011 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
