/E (The main determinant of the base weight of each disclosure item was the frequency with which it appeared across the entire knowledge base \(doc_coverage_pct\). However, the frequency within the source documents in their respective clusters \(cluster_frequency_rank\) was also taken into consideration. The two ranks are combined with a 75/25 weighting, respectively, to obtain a frequency score:)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1189 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 701 0 R
/E (frequency_score = 0.75\(doc_coverage_pct\)+0.25\(cluster_frequency_rank\))
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1190 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 701 0 R
/E (This produces a composite score between 0 and 1, where 1 indicates that the indicator is in both the highest-coverage and highest-frequency clusters within its evidence pool. This specific weighting choice reflects a deliberate methodological principle: a concept supported by fifteen different organisations, each mentioning it once, is stronger evidence of importance than one organisation mentioning it fifteen times. The higher weight on breadth prevents any single prolific source \(for example, the GHG Protocol, which contributes many granular atomic indicators for various GHG calculation sub-steps\) from artificially inflating the weight of GHG reporting relative to other concepts. This is consistent with best practices in systematic review methodology, where independent replication across studies is treated as more evidentially significant than depth within a single study \(Higgins et al., 2023\).)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
