2159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 743 0 R
/E (The approach is consistent with established practice in survey instrument design, where Likert-type and ordinal scales are routinely converted to numeric scores for aggregation, provided the interval assumptions of the conversion are made explicit \(Carifio & Perla, 2008\). One design choice worth noting is that the mapping for the three-option categorical scale assigns exactly 50 to the middle option, which encodes an assumption that partial implementation is precisely halfway between no implementation and full implementation. This is a simplification. In reality, a company that has a supplier code of conduct but does not enforce it may be closer to 0 than to 50, while one that enforces it informally without documentation may be closer to 100. The equal-interval mapping is a pragmatic compromise that sacrifices granularity for consistency and ease of completion, recognising that SMEs without dedicated sustainability staff are unlikely to be able to make finer-grained self-assessments reliably. Future iterations could consider behaviorally anchored rating scales \(BARS\), which provide concrete descriptions for each scale point, as a mechanism for improving calibration without increasing respondent burden \(Smith & Kendall, 1963\).)
/Pg 82 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
