2165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 744 0 R
/E (A critical implementation detail applies to percentage questions that feed into exponential decay or benchmark-ratio methods \(described below\): the raw input, which is entered as a whole number \(e.g., 14 for 14%\), is divided by 100 before the formula is applied, and the benchmark parameter is similarly adjusted if it was stored as a whole-number percentage. This ensures dimensional consistency between the input and the formula's internal parameters.)
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
2166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 744 0 R
/T (Numeric Responses: The Normalization Challenge)
/E (Numeric Responses: The Normalization Challenge)
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 744 0 R
/E (Numeric responses represent the most methodologically complex normalization challenge in the system. Unlike boolean or categorical responses, numeric inputs can range across many orders of magnitude depending on the SME's size, sector, and reporting boundaries. A company with 10 employees and one with 10,000 employees might both be high-performing on energy management, but their absolute energy consumption figures will differ by orders of magnitude. Normalizing based on absolute numbers would systematically advantage small companies on resource consumption metrics and disadvantage them on metrics where scale is intrinsically positive \(e.g., number of employees trained\). The system addresses this through a combination of intensity conversion, benchmark anchoring, and method-specific normalization formulas, each matched to the underlying sustainability concept being measured.)
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 744 0 R
/T (Linear Cap \(Higher is Better\): )
/E (Linear Cap \(Higher is Better\): )
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 744 0 R
/E (For questions where a higher raw value indicates better performance and where there is a natural ceiling beyond which further improvement adds no additional procurement-relevant signal, a linear cap normalization is applied. The cap value \(param_1\) is set to the threshold above which a score of 100 is awarded. Below the cap, the score is computed as:)
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 744 0 R
/E <6E6F726D616C697A65645F73636F7265203D206D696E2878202F20706172616D5F312C20312920D720313030>
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 744 0 R
/E (For example, if the cap for annual safety training hours per employee is set at 8 hours, a company providing 4 hours receives a score of 50; one providing 8 or more hours receives 100. This method is appropriate when the concept exhibits diminishing returns to sustainability performance beyond a threshold. In this example, additional training hours beyond a certain point add marginal benefit relative to the procurement risk they address. The cap value is set based on industry norms and framework guidance rather than arbitrarily, and is documented in the MetricBenchmarks table alongside its source.)
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
