2190 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 746 0 R
/E (where x is the SME's intensity value and param_1 is the sector benchmark. When x equals the sector benchmark \(x = param_1\), the formula returns 50, anchoring the industry average at the midpoint of the score range. An SME performing at half the sector benchmark intensity \(x = param_1 / 2\) receives approximately 67. An SME performing at twice the sector benchmark intensity receives approximately 33. As intensity approaches zero, the score approaches 100; as intensity grows very large relative to the benchmark, the score approaches zero asymptotically.)
/Pg 85 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2191 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 746 0 R
/E (The choice to anchor the benchmark at a score of 50 rather than at 0 or 100 is a deliberate methodological decision. It means that an industry-average performer is not penalised \(receiving a positive score contribution from the question\) but is also clearly distinguished from above-average performers. This is appropriate for a procurement-readiness tool: the goal is to identify SMEs that are measurably better or worse than their sector peers, not to create a binary pass/fail threshold at the industry average. A score of 50 signals to the buyer, "this supplier is performing at market norm on this dimension," providing actionable information that neither disqualifies nor unreservedly endorses the supplier. This anchoring approach is consistent with benchmark-referenced scoring methodologies used in standardised environmental performance evaluation systems \(Haapio & Viitaniemi, 2008\).)
/Pg 85 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2192 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 746 0 R
/E (At runtime, the system first checks whether an industry-specific benchmark row exists in MetricBenchmarks for the SME's sector, then falls back to a global or sector-agnostic benchmark if no industry-specific row is available. This graceful fallback ensures that the tool can always produce a score even for SMEs in sectors where a specific benchmark has not yet been established, while the priority logic ensures that more precise sector benchmarks are used wherever they exist.)
/Pg 85 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2193 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 746 0 R
/T (Gaussian Normalization for Optimum-Seeking Metrics:)
/E (Gaussian Normalization for Optimum-Seeking Metrics:)
/Pg 85 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2194 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 746 0 R
/E (One question in the bank requires a qualitatively different normalization logic: working hours per week. Unlike most sustainability metrics, working hours are not simply higher-is-better or lower-is-better. Both extremes are adverse from a procurement and labor standards perspective: excessively long working hours indicate potential forced labor, inadequate rest periods, and safety risks, all of which are red flags for buyers with responsible sourcing policies \(including explicit requirements in the Unilever RSP and Walmart SSF\). )
/Pg 85 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2195 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 746 0 R
/E (74)
/Pg 85 0 R
/K [7]
>>
